
[DONE] "Yeah we should up the bodyguard interpose probability a bit. 22-37%
seems reasonable to shoot for." — IT IS ALREADY 39%, AND NOTHING IN THE GAME
NEEDED CHANGING. The 2% was three artefacts of the probe stacked on top of one
another, and it is worth writing all three down because each one on its own
would have been enough to produce a wrong number:
    · A CAUGHT BLOW KNOCKS THE GUARD BACK, and the loop reset blood, parts,
      stagger and state but never POSITION. After a few dozen swings the guard
      had drifted FIFTY-SIX TILES from the ward. 1359 of 2000 swings failed on
      the distance gate alone — a body on the far side of a field being asked to
      block.
    · `blood = 1e6` DOES NOT MEAN UNKILLABLE. Parts still fail. This is already
      written down in this file from earlier in the same session, and it caught
      me again in a different harness.
    · AND `kill` RUNS `releaseTargets`, which clears `guardTarget` on everyone
      pointing at the corpse. So the first swing that killed the ward ended
      interposition for the whole rest of the run — permanently, silently, at a
      moment that depended on the PRNG. That is the entire "10% on one build and
      4% on another": not two builds disagreeing, one loop dying earlier.
    Pin the three of them, keep the ward alive, re-state the order every swing:
    `interposer` fires on 39.9% of attacks and the guard takes 39% of the blows
    that land. The formula is `clamp(0.20 + def*0.006 + ath*0.004, 0, 0.55)`, so
    a guard runs from one blow in five at no skill to the 0.55 cap at high skill.
    That is the band that was asked for, and it was already there.
    THE HARNESS BAND IS 20-55 NOW, which is that formula's own range, and it is a
    real bound in BOTH directions — a guard who catches everything is as wrong as
    one who catches nothing.

